1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:06,140
And the seat of the former Soviet Union is the hub of one of the largest railroad networks in the world.

2
00:00:07,300 --> 00:00:13,760
There are nine railway stations in Moscow that route a million passengers a day to and from Europe and Asia.

3
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:20,720
In this vast land, trains carry more passengers than any other means of transportation.

4
00:00:23,360 --> 00:00:29,620
Yaroslavsky Station, built under Joseph Stalin, is the western terminus for the most famous train in the country.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:32,000
the Trans-Siberian Railway.

6
00:00:32,001 --> 00:00:36,000
It makes 45 stops over an eight-day trip

7
00:00:36,001 --> 00:00:42,000
that runs 6,183 miles over, across, and through a continent

8
00:00:42,001 --> 00:00:46,000
synonymous with frigid temperatures and impassable terrain.

9
00:00:47,000 --> 00:00:51,000
Along the way, it is everything a train can be.

10
00:00:51,001 --> 00:00:54,000
A school bus, a taxi, a job.

11
00:00:54,001 --> 00:01:03,000
Most of all, it is an essential link to villages like Litsvianca on the fringe of civilization

12
00:01:03,001 --> 00:01:04,842
in the heart of Central Asia.

13
00:01:04,843 --> 00:01:08,400
To the people who depend on this train, there is nothing

14
00:01:08,401 --> 00:01:13,620
extraordinary about the sight of tracks and tunnels carved through the sheer granite cliffs

15
00:01:13,621 --> 00:01:19,720
that rise 1,500 feet above Lake Baikal, where ice lingers till May and temperatures drop

16
00:01:19,721 --> 00:01:24,970
to minus 40 in February. The railroad has been here since 1905.

17
00:01:24,971 --> 00:01:26,720
No one remembers life

18
00:01:26,940 --> 00:01:31,929
without it. Like a heartbeat, it is quietly taken for granted.

19
00:01:31,930 --> 00:01:33,940
Yet a century ago, it was

20
00:01:37,540 --> 00:01:41,755
virtually unthinkable that a train would unite Europe and Asia.

21
00:01:41,756 --> 00:01:43,160
Here, the terrain and

22
00:01:43,161 --> 00:01:48,609
climate are treacherous. The job was enormous, and the costs were staggering.

23
00:01:48,610 --> 00:01:49,600
The modern-day

24
00:01:49,601 --> 00:01:56,360
equivalent of $50 billion, nearly twice that of landing a man on the moon to build a railroad

25
00:01:56,361 --> 00:01:58,756
through a land just as desolate.

26
00:01:58,757 --> 00:02:03,100
In spite of the obstacles, by the end of the 19th century,

27
00:02:03,101 --> 00:02:06,280
it was a national imperative to build it.

28
00:02:06,281 --> 00:02:12,840
It sometimes took years for messages and edicts and plans to reach the farthest edges of their

29
00:02:12,841 --> 00:02:19,660
empire without lines of communication, without an effective internal telegraph system, without

30
00:02:19,661 --> 00:02:22,400
a railroad system, Russia was at a serious disadvantage.

31
00:02:23,400 --> 00:02:27,772
The country occupies the largest area on earth. It stretches from St.

32
00:02:27,773 --> 00:02:29,420
Petersburg to the Pacific.

33
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:35,400
It is wider than the breadth of the United States and Canada combined, but only wagon

34
00:02:35,401 --> 00:02:37,120
trails had linked it to the capital.

35
00:02:37,121 --> 00:02:44,120
Russia needed a railroad to command and control its vast Asian territories.

36
00:02:44,121 --> 00:02:50,120
There were constant reports that China planned to overrun Siberia's undefended border.

37
00:02:50,121 --> 00:02:54,120
Warships from Japan menaced the Russian coast.

38
00:02:54,121 --> 00:03:01,120
Even more troubling were Siberian revolutionaries who demonstrated their desire of breaking away from the capital.

39
00:03:01,121 --> 00:03:06,120
By the reign of Alexander III, Russia was adrift in crisis.

40
00:03:07,120 --> 00:03:12,580
Czar Alexander III came into power when his father was assassinated in 1881.

41
00:03:13,580 --> 00:03:20,680
His entire reign was dedicated to the idea of Russia for the Russians,

42
00:03:20,880 --> 00:03:24,440
of establishing firmer governmental control over a country

43
00:03:24,441 --> 00:03:26,980
that seemed to be slipping out of control.

44
00:03:27,740 --> 00:03:30,020
The railroad was the answer to all of this.

45
00:03:30,700 --> 00:03:36,100
It was partly military defense, but it was also internal police measures.

46
00:03:36,101 --> 00:03:43,100
Alexander III was determined to build a railroad to move soldiers to and from the hinterland.

47
00:03:43,101 --> 00:03:47,100
But he also intended to use it to colonize Siberia.

48
00:03:47,101 --> 00:03:53,100
Just as the American Transcontinental Railroad had tamed the West, Russia would tame the East.

49
00:03:53,101 --> 00:03:57,100
There was nothing but scattered forts and trading posts.

50
00:03:57,101 --> 00:04:02,100
Few people lived here except convicts in exile and Stone Age tribes.

51
00:04:02,101 --> 00:04:09,100
The Tsar wanted the same success as America's transcontinental railroad, but had none of the economic foundation.

52
00:04:09,101 --> 00:04:19,100
One of the most profound disadvantages that Russia faced in this project was a lack of entrepreneurial tradition.

53
00:04:19,101 --> 00:04:24,061
This was not a challenge of technology. They didn't have to invent anything new.

54
00:04:24,062 --> 00:04:27,100
It was a challenge of financing and organization.

55
00:04:27,101 --> 00:04:30,100
Russia was a feudal society.

56
00:04:30,101 --> 00:04:36,100
Its railroad building experience was limited to a few hundred miles of track with antiquated trains.

57
00:04:36,101 --> 00:04:42,100
It's one thing to project a railroad of 500 or even 1,000 miles.

58
00:04:42,101 --> 00:04:48,100
It's quite another to project a railroad that would consume unimaginable quantities of timber,

59
00:04:48,101 --> 00:04:52,100
require hundreds of bridges, thousands of miles of rail,

60
00:04:52,101 --> 00:04:55,100
millions of tons of iron for spikes and bolts.

61
00:04:55,101 --> 00:04:55,801
and bolts.

62
00:04:55,802 --> 00:05:00,980
Even running a few trains daily on a line 6,000 miles long is going to require

63
00:05:00,981 --> 00:05:05,880
thousands of cars, hundreds of locomotives, and training a workforce in a country that

64
00:05:05,881 --> 00:05:10,020
didn't have a great reservoir of mechanical talent.

65
00:05:10,500 --> 00:05:16,580
No railroad in Russia or the world is as long or as complicated as the one called for in

66
00:05:16,581 --> 00:05:17,741
the Trans-Siberian.

67
00:05:17,742 --> 00:05:22,140
It had to cross three of the world's largest rivers, the world's largest

68
00:05:22,141 --> 00:05:26,909
forest, the world's largest lake in the world's worst climate.

69
00:05:26,910 --> 00:05:29,140
Much of the railway was built

70
00:05:29,141 --> 00:05:30,238
on permafrost.

71
00:05:30,239 --> 00:05:36,040
It is rock solid most of the year but melts into craters under the stress.

72
00:05:36,041 --> 00:05:39,202
They must also tunnel through a mountain chain.

73
00:05:39,203 --> 00:05:41,960
Overall, there are countless bridges over

74
00:05:41,961 --> 00:05:45,420
ravines and rivers.

75
00:05:45,421 --> 00:05:55,820
The Trans-Siberian Railroad was simply one of the most grandiose enterprises of the 19th century,

76
00:05:55,821 --> 00:06:02,820
maybe of all of modern history, more costly than any other peaceful undertaking in the history of the modern world.

77
00:06:02,821 --> 00:06:10,820
Alexander entrusted the Committee of Communication with the task of planning the railroad.

78
00:06:10,821 --> 00:06:16,060
But the committee was stymied. They advised the Tsar against it.

79
00:06:16,560 --> 00:06:21,520
They didn't see that the government could afford to build this railroad, and they were right.

80
00:06:22,000 --> 00:06:26,880
There wasn't a labor force of an adequate size in isolated Siberia.

81
00:06:27,180 --> 00:06:30,806
There weren't steel mills in Siberia to supply the railroad.

82
00:06:30,807 --> 00:06:32,680
There weren't supplies of coal.

83
00:06:33,520 --> 00:06:37,520
Impossible or not, the Tsar was determined to have a railroad.

84
00:06:37,521 --> 00:06:42,340
When the committee told him it couldn't be built, he dissolved the committee.

85
00:06:43,140 --> 00:06:50,900
Then entrusted Sergei Vita, the builder of the 600-mile Odessa Line, to build the 6,000-mile Trans-Siberian.

86
00:06:51,620 --> 00:06:56,120
But the Tsar was naive to think a royal temper could assure success.

87
00:06:57,640 --> 00:07:00,400
A railroad is much more than trains and track.

88
00:07:00,401 --> 00:07:04,620
It's not a thing. It's nothing that a czar can call into being

89
00:07:04,621 --> 00:07:10,060
and then expect to work like some machine that you throw a switch and it goes forward.

90
00:07:10,280 --> 00:07:17,200
It's a complex interaction of human beings and parts and pieces extending over thousands of miles.

91
00:07:18,420 --> 00:07:24,200
The longest railroad in the world today and the biggest project of the 19th century

92
00:07:24,201 --> 00:07:27,280
began in a cloud of bureaucratic fear.

93
00:07:28,040 --> 00:07:30,840
While planners searched for a miracle to build it,

94
00:07:31,440 --> 00:07:34,120
the Tsar boasted it was Russia's road to power,

95
00:07:34,760 --> 00:07:37,180
the start of a new era of peace and prosperity.

96
00:07:38,100 --> 00:07:42,820
The fact was, it would provoke a war and nearly bankrupt the country.

97
00:07:43,840 --> 00:07:48,820
As the century ended, many wondered if the railroad was worth its enormous expense,

98
00:07:48,821 --> 00:07:50,820
nearly two-thirds the state budget,

99
00:07:51,220 --> 00:07:56,680
or if it was just a desperate gamble of a dynasty in its waning days to stay in power.

100
00:07:57,280 --> 00:08:04,280
Midnight in Moscow marks the departure of the Trans-Siberian Railroad.

101
00:08:04,281 --> 00:08:11,280
A first-class ticket costs $1,200 for the eight-day trip to Vladivostok,

102
00:08:11,281 --> 00:08:15,280
but many travel and sleep in berths for under $600.

103
00:08:15,281 --> 00:08:20,280
By dawn, the train reaches the Volga and crosses Europe's longest river.

104
00:08:20,281 --> 00:08:26,280
On the distant bank is Yaroslav and its 900-year-old cathedral.

105
00:08:26,281 --> 00:08:31,120
From here, the train continues on, bound for Siberia and beyond.

106
00:08:32,980 --> 00:08:37,560
Trains and train centers like this were once the primary target of German aircraft

107
00:08:37,561 --> 00:08:40,740
trying to cut supply lines to the front in World War II.

108
00:08:45,260 --> 00:08:50,600
But Yaroslav and the Trans-Siberian were spared because they were out of range.

109
00:08:51,540 --> 00:08:54,040
In war, distance protected the train.

110
00:08:54,041 --> 00:08:58,920
Yet in building it, distance nearly destroyed it.

111
00:08:58,921 --> 00:09:05,540
Its remote location and sheer length drove the cost so high, the project was almost derailed

112
00:09:05,541 --> 00:09:09,040
before it began.

113
00:09:09,041 --> 00:09:14,520
I'm sure that the Russians wanted to emulate the best, but as they began to price out this

114
00:09:14,521 --> 00:09:19,340
huge long thing, which was maybe three times longer than any transcontinental railroad

115
00:09:19,341 --> 00:09:24,540
ever built before, it quickly became apparent they'd bankrupt the treasury and they only

116
00:09:24,541 --> 00:09:26,240
get halfway.

117
00:09:26,241 --> 00:09:26,941
In St.

118
00:09:26,942 --> 00:09:30,720
Petersburg, the railroad committee was grappling with the most difficult detail

119
00:09:30,721 --> 00:09:33,720
of all, money.

120
00:09:33,721 --> 00:09:39,360
Sergey Vita, the project's point man, had to find a way to build the world's longest railroad

121
00:09:39,361 --> 00:09:40,860
on a budget.

122
00:09:40,861 --> 00:09:46,160
He had no alternative but to cut the costs of materials and labor and collapse the project's

123
00:09:46,161 --> 00:09:48,500
time to completion.

124
00:09:48,501 --> 00:09:53,940
The best railroads used a double track made of steel rails that weighed 90 pounds to the yard.

125
00:09:54,480 --> 00:10:00,520
He would have to build a single line instead, with rails made of iron that only weighed 49 pounds.

126
00:10:01,140 --> 00:10:04,000
The measure reduced costs 75%.

127
00:10:04,001 --> 00:10:10,360
Next, timber, not stone and steel, was to be used in all but the widest river crossings.

128
00:10:10,800 --> 00:10:13,000
The work had to be done by hand.

129
00:10:13,560 --> 00:10:15,740
There was no money for dynamite or machines.

130
00:10:15,741 --> 00:10:18,500
and there could be no costly surveys.

131
00:10:19,160 --> 00:10:22,140
The royal directive was, just do it.

132
00:10:22,920 --> 00:10:25,380
The government was so desperate,

133
00:10:25,720 --> 00:10:30,800
it was almost policy to get it done as quickly as possible,

134
00:10:31,300 --> 00:10:33,260
as cheaply as possible,

135
00:10:34,080 --> 00:10:38,600
but at the same time, whatever it took was the rule.

136
00:10:40,500 --> 00:10:45,160
In March 1891, church bells heralded the 10th anniversary

137
00:10:45,161 --> 00:10:47,340
of Alexander's ascension to the throne.

138
00:10:48,240 --> 00:10:50,140
It was also the day the Tsar marked

139
00:10:50,141 --> 00:10:53,160
to start building the railroad, ready or not.

140
00:10:54,240 --> 00:10:57,020
In Vladivostok, the Tsarevich Nicholas arrived

141
00:10:57,021 --> 00:10:59,260
to shovel the first spade full of dirt,

142
00:10:59,880 --> 00:11:01,740
then attended a gala celebration

143
00:11:01,741 --> 00:11:03,600
honoring the project's engineers.

144
00:11:04,420 --> 00:11:07,900
Amid music and champagne, the railroad was underway.

145
00:11:09,060 --> 00:11:12,340
Back at the capital, Vita mapped out the master plan

146
00:11:12,341 --> 00:11:14,640
to complete the railroad within 10 years,

147
00:11:14,641 --> 00:11:16,160
as the Tsar had ordered.

148
00:11:17,320 --> 00:11:19,760
With the construction season limited to four months

149
00:11:19,761 --> 00:11:21,240
by the sub-zero winters,

150
00:11:21,920 --> 00:11:25,120
Vita had 40 months to build 6,000 miles of track,

151
00:11:25,500 --> 00:11:27,420
or 150 miles a day.

152
00:11:28,120 --> 00:11:29,920
The prospect was impossible,

153
00:11:30,580 --> 00:11:34,200
so he borrowed an idea from Canada's Transcontinental Railroad

154
00:11:34,201 --> 00:11:36,760
to build in sections simultaneously.

155
00:11:37,780 --> 00:11:40,840
Vita broke the Trans-Siberian down into six parts,

156
00:11:41,220 --> 00:11:43,020
intending to build them all at once.

157
00:11:43,021 --> 00:11:50,580
If all went according to plan, he only needed to build about 25 miles of track per month on each section to succeed.

158
00:11:51,460 --> 00:11:58,600
To further speed the project, he scheduled bridge and tunnel construction in winter, when crews wouldn't have to pickaxe frozen earth.

159
00:11:59,820 --> 00:12:06,680
The first segment to get started was the Usuri line, joining Haberask to Vladivostok on the Pacific coast.

160
00:12:08,000 --> 00:12:10,520
It was the country's most urgent link.

161
00:12:10,521 --> 00:12:16,060
The army needed the train to shuttle soldiers along the Pacific coastline to guard against invasion.

162
00:12:17,200 --> 00:12:22,060
Supply ships reached the distant crews after a 40-day voyage from the Baltic to the Pacific.

163
00:12:23,840 --> 00:12:28,420
There were horrendous delays, but the real problem was labor.

164
00:12:30,580 --> 00:12:35,580
Native Siberian tribes refused to work, calling the railway the Iron Scar.

165
00:12:35,581 --> 00:12:42,460
Officials tried to recruit workers from Russian cities, but none were enticed by the offer

166
00:12:42,461 --> 00:12:47,500
of free room and board and 12 cents a day to work in Siberia.

167
00:12:47,501 --> 00:12:51,480
The government was desperate for able-bodied men.

168
00:12:51,481 --> 00:12:55,000
When we built our transcontinental railroad right after the Civil War, a lot of guys are,

169
00:12:55,001 --> 00:12:58,200
you know, mustered out looking for employment.

170
00:12:58,201 --> 00:13:00,060
And wages weren't too bad.

171
00:13:00,061 --> 00:13:04,820
But in Siberia, you can't afford to pay these people a lot.

172
00:13:04,821 --> 00:13:09,580
So you end up with a lot of people, I think, that were either prisoners or economically conscripted.

173
00:13:10,880 --> 00:13:13,940
The bulk of the workforce was Russian convicts.

174
00:13:14,440 --> 00:13:18,180
They were barefoot in summer and dressed in thick layers for the spring and fall.

175
00:13:18,960 --> 00:13:20,900
A shaved right head indicated a murderer.

176
00:13:23,260 --> 00:13:26,920
Beside convicts are free men from China, Persia, and Turkey.

177
00:13:27,500 --> 00:13:29,680
The pay was 12 cents a day.

178
00:13:30,640 --> 00:13:33,160
Stone masons recruited from Italy earned a dollar.

179
00:13:34,160 --> 00:13:36,920
Other than work, the men either ate or slept.

180
00:13:37,640 --> 00:13:38,980
The food was dismal.

181
00:13:39,860 --> 00:13:44,460
Cooks slaughtered pack animals too weak to work to make stews of mule and horse meat.

182
00:13:45,380 --> 00:13:48,560
At night, men slept in tents or crude bunkhouses.

183
00:13:49,920 --> 00:13:53,120
The convicts were shackled to wheelbarrows to prevent their escape.

184
00:13:53,800 --> 00:13:57,900
At first light, everyone was back to the tracks until dark,

185
00:13:58,460 --> 00:14:01,720
usually at 10 p.m. in the northern latitudes of Siberia.

186
00:14:01,721 --> 00:14:07,020
The routine was repeated until the winter suspended work till spring.

187
00:14:07,920 --> 00:14:12,100
In the chains of Siberian prisoners, the railroad found the miracle it needed.

188
00:14:13,040 --> 00:14:17,420
The enemies of the state became the builders of the state's most important undertaking.

189
00:14:18,240 --> 00:14:23,900
Motivation consisted of unshackling a prisoner and taking a day off his sentence for a day of labor.

190
00:14:23,901 --> 00:14:31,280
They were actually paid fairly well, and they were fed fairly well, and conditions were much better.

191
00:14:31,720 --> 00:14:35,800
working on the railroad than they were rotting away inside of a prison.

192
00:14:37,000 --> 00:14:40,440
Shackles or not, the prisoners often slipped into town,

193
00:14:40,660 --> 00:14:44,040
bringing outrage from the local governor for the mayhem they caused.

194
00:14:44,760 --> 00:14:47,920
But the railroad officials were less concerned with conduct

195
00:14:47,921 --> 00:14:50,860
and more troubled by the fact that they needed more bodies

196
00:14:50,861 --> 00:14:53,480
to work every hour of daylight to the maximum.

197
00:14:56,700 --> 00:14:59,100
Imperial soldiers were ordered into service,

198
00:14:59,101 --> 00:15:02,220
but refused to work beside criminals and went on strike.

199
00:15:03,020 --> 00:15:06,820
Finally, the government invited 15,000 coolies from China.

200
00:15:07,820 --> 00:15:10,720
The government's motley labor force pushed on.

201
00:15:11,120 --> 00:15:12,800
Construction moved at full speed,

202
00:15:13,180 --> 00:15:16,040
but by late spring, it was completely disrupted.

203
00:15:16,740 --> 00:15:19,960
The initial surveys failed to reveal a critical detail.

204
00:15:20,480 --> 00:15:23,600
The melting rivers, fed by tons of winter ice and snow,

205
00:15:24,020 --> 00:15:27,220
had overrun their banks by as much as 15 miles.

206
00:15:27,221 --> 00:15:35,880
In many cases, the engineers who were assigned to survey this 5,000 or 6,000 miles through what was often a trackless wilderness,

207
00:15:37,140 --> 00:15:44,160
apparently often would decide that they just really didn't have the time to run every line and figure out every curve.

208
00:15:44,840 --> 00:15:48,920
So that in many cases, the surveys were, shall we say, imaginary.

209
00:15:49,520 --> 00:15:55,500
Lines drawn on maps without an engineer ever setting foot in that particular forest or that particular step.

210
00:15:55,501 --> 00:16:05,484
Well, by the time the track layers get there, they encounter features like rivers or streams or hills that the surveyors had overlooked because they'd never been there

211
00:16:05,485 --> 00:16:06,620
in the first place.

212
00:16:06,980 --> 00:16:11,240
The crew hammered away at the route's 470 arduous miles.

213
00:16:12,000 --> 00:16:13,620
But there were more obstacles.

214
00:16:14,500 --> 00:16:18,080
The floods turned into swamps that bred disease.

215
00:16:19,680 --> 00:16:22,200
Siberian anthrax decimated the pack animals.

216
00:16:22,201 --> 00:16:26,200
mosquitoes transmitted fevers that incapacitated hundreds.

217
00:16:26,201 --> 00:16:31,200
Worst of all was an insect barely visible to the human eye.

218
00:16:31,201 --> 00:16:37,200
The Siberian gnats alone would be enough to make most modern human beings go crazy.

219
00:16:37,201 --> 00:16:41,200
They were thick, unrelenting, they would get into your clothes.

220
00:16:41,201 --> 00:16:47,200
They were a combination of vicious flea and nasty mosquito rolled up into one,

221
00:16:47,201 --> 00:16:50,200
and there were billions of them.

222
00:16:50,201 --> 00:16:56,820
It became the practice of Siberian railroad workers to literally dress themselves in netting.

223
00:16:57,820 --> 00:17:00,820
Whatever the challenges, Vita's plan was succeeding.

224
00:17:01,600 --> 00:17:04,920
The real problem was still the old problem, money.

225
00:17:05,700 --> 00:17:08,520
Even with cost cuts, the project needed more.

226
00:17:09,340 --> 00:17:15,160
To raise more funds, he kept the news about the railroad's difficulty and delays, a state secret.

227
00:17:15,161 --> 00:17:21,620
The Trans-Siberian was accompanied by a major propaganda campaign at the world's fairs of the day,

228
00:17:21,920 --> 00:17:28,060
especially intended for French and other foreign investors to see that the country was coming alive.

229
00:17:29,140 --> 00:17:34,680
Investors heard the government stress all the financial advantages of a railroad linking Europe to Asia.

230
00:17:35,460 --> 00:17:39,340
They were shown the most opulent interiors of any railroad cars in the world,

231
00:17:39,940 --> 00:17:43,260
and told the richest would ride these rails across Asia.

232
00:17:43,261 --> 00:17:53,960
There was no mention of the fact the railroad was built on substandard ties and tracks with prison labor and traveled at best 15 miles an hour.

233
00:17:55,000 --> 00:18:00,240
The scheme worked. The French added up fresh loans, but it was still not enough.

234
00:18:01,160 --> 00:18:04,660
Vita ordered the treasury to print as much money as the project needed.

235
00:18:05,380 --> 00:18:08,020
Whatever the debt, the railroad would pay it off.

236
00:18:08,840 --> 00:18:12,080
All Vita had to do was finish it fast.

237
00:18:13,260 --> 00:18:26,620
Five days after leaving Moscow, the Trans-Siberian Railroad reaches the western bank of the Angara River and the city of Irkutsk, 2800 miles away.

238
00:18:27,420 --> 00:18:31,540
It is the halfway point of the journey east and the largest city in Siberia.

239
00:18:33,760 --> 00:18:38,640
Irkutsk is the crossroads of Central Asia for passengers going to Mongolia and China.

240
00:18:38,641 --> 00:18:45,640
The arrival of the Trans-Siberian a century ago made the city a trade center.

241
00:18:45,641 --> 00:18:53,640
The homes of rich merchants and the churches they endowed still stand.

242
00:18:53,641 --> 00:18:57,640
Near the train station is the city's World War II memorial.

243
00:18:57,641 --> 00:18:59,640
It's a fitting location.

244
00:18:59,641 --> 00:19:07,640
With the Nazi invasion, the Trans-Siberian became a war horse, moving whole armies to the front.

245
00:19:07,641 --> 00:19:09,757
In peace, it is a workhorse.

246
00:19:09,758 --> 00:19:16,940
The Trans-Siberian carries the industrial lifeblood of coal, oil and gas from Asia to the West.

247
00:19:17,600 --> 00:19:21,676
The tracks are built to bear the weight of 100 loaded boxcars.

248
00:19:21,677 --> 00:19:25,160
Its engines can reach top speeds of 70 miles an hour.

249
00:19:26,160 --> 00:19:34,680
The speed and strength of the Trans-Siberian in the 21st century is the stuff of dreams to the railroad committee who built it in 1891.

250
00:19:37,640 --> 00:19:44,640
For Sergei Vita, building cheap was a necessity, building quick a strategy.

251
00:19:44,641 --> 00:19:48,640
The sooner the railroad was finished, the sooner it could earn money.

252
00:19:48,641 --> 00:19:55,640
Money needed to upgrade the tracks and railbed that, by necessity, had to be cheap to cover 6,000 miles.

253
00:19:55,641 --> 00:20:03,640
The linchpin in the strategy was concurrent construction, building the Trans-Siberian in sections all at once.

254
00:20:03,641 --> 00:20:10,020
The biggest challenge in crossing the Earth's largest land mass was not the land, but the water.

255
00:20:10,400 --> 00:20:16,000
The supply line for building the railroad ideally comes from both ends.

256
00:20:16,320 --> 00:20:20,960
The trains just bring it in on the tracks you've already built, and that's the best way to carry it.

257
00:20:21,560 --> 00:20:26,060
But the Trans-Siberian Railway was started at six different points,

258
00:20:26,720 --> 00:20:30,380
so what they did was bring supplies in by boat,

259
00:20:30,381 --> 00:20:35,500
which meant going up to the Arctic Sea and then coming up one of the rivers.

260
00:20:37,160 --> 00:20:42,040
Ships were slated to drop off construction supplies to crews building east and west,

261
00:20:42,200 --> 00:20:43,920
but they never made it.

262
00:20:44,660 --> 00:20:49,420
The Arctic Sea was a killer, unlike anything the railroad committee expected.

263
00:20:50,420 --> 00:20:52,540
The first supply flotilla was shipwrecked.

264
00:20:53,120 --> 00:20:56,420
Thousands of miles of tracks and tons of spikes were lost.

265
00:20:56,421 --> 00:20:58,895
The accident set construction back.

266
00:20:58,896 --> 00:21:04,340
Six months after the project began, it was already two years behind schedule.

267
00:21:05,460 --> 00:21:07,760
The Arctic Sea was dropped as a supply route.

268
00:21:08,640 --> 00:21:13,680
The project had to wait for slow-traveling supply trains arriving on provisional tracks.

269
00:21:14,480 --> 00:21:17,780
Delayed but not defeated, the work pushed on.

270
00:21:19,060 --> 00:21:23,760
A year after the Arctic Sea debacle, the project was resupplied with rails.

271
00:21:24,760 --> 00:21:29,640
Crews at the westernmost edge of Siberia forged eastward across the Barabinskaya steppe.

272
00:21:30,680 --> 00:21:38,040
In stretches of permafrost, crews built innumerable bonfires to melt the ice-hard soil and sink the track into solid ground.

273
00:21:39,120 --> 00:21:42,320
Dynamite would have made it easier, but it wasn't in the budget.

274
00:21:42,620 --> 00:21:49,380
The work of most Siberian track builders was repetitive and very difficult.

275
00:21:49,381 --> 00:21:55,520
They didn't have steam shovels, they didn't have access to what we would regard as modern hand tools.

276
00:21:57,020 --> 00:22:00,580
They were shaping the earth in the profile for the track,

277
00:22:01,520 --> 00:22:06,240
and doing so with wooden shovels, with rakes, with small light picks.

278
00:22:06,760 --> 00:22:09,080
And in some cases, they didn't even have wheelbarrows.

279
00:22:09,320 --> 00:22:13,520
They would drag the soil on barrows to where they needed it.

280
00:22:13,521 --> 00:22:19,500
On September 11, 1894, three and a half years after the start of the project,

281
00:22:19,960 --> 00:22:21,280
crews reached the Aub River.

282
00:22:21,880 --> 00:22:23,900
It is one of the world's largest rivers.

283
00:22:25,120 --> 00:22:29,460
While crews with supplies ferried across the river to continue building east,

284
00:22:29,920 --> 00:22:31,040
masons began work.

285
00:22:33,000 --> 00:22:34,900
Slowly, the bridges took shape,

286
00:22:34,901 --> 00:22:38,540
with stone piers and thick buttresses that slanted outward

287
00:22:38,541 --> 00:22:43,520
to deflect the tons of ice that swept downriver at a crushing 6 miles per hour.

288
00:22:44,220 --> 00:22:46,980
But the upper section of the bridge was delayed for months.

289
00:22:48,020 --> 00:22:50,800
Every stick of rail, every nut and bolt for the bridges,

290
00:22:51,080 --> 00:22:56,120
every piece of metal had to be hauled in from workshops in Moscow or Warsaw

291
00:22:56,121 --> 00:22:58,520
or other parts of European Russia.

292
00:22:59,340 --> 00:23:03,700
The bridge beams, bearings, and rivets were packaged into hundreds of boxcars

293
00:23:03,701 --> 00:23:06,340
and shipped over months like pieces of a puzzle.

294
00:23:07,340 --> 00:23:10,800
The supply trains traveled at speeds no better than a walking pace.

295
00:23:12,220 --> 00:23:14,340
Bridge construction took place through the winter.

296
00:23:15,160 --> 00:23:18,080
It was a ruthless schedule with deadly consequences.

297
00:23:19,120 --> 00:23:22,620
But you've got problems there with working in the extreme cold.

298
00:23:23,220 --> 00:23:25,160
Even with gloves, your hands get cold.

299
00:23:25,600 --> 00:23:29,180
You step back to do something and you miss your step on this bridge.

300
00:23:29,181 --> 00:23:32,340
You grab for something and it's not there or your hand isn't working.

301
00:23:32,900 --> 00:23:34,720
All of a sudden, there you are down in the river.

302
00:23:34,721 --> 00:23:38,200
And it's not a gentle splash that season of the year. It's a thud.

303
00:23:38,660 --> 00:23:44,240
The Russians stoically accepted the loss of 10 men for every river crossing and pushed on.

304
00:23:44,940 --> 00:23:49,820
After the bridge was finished came the real dangerous job, testing it.

305
00:23:50,620 --> 00:23:55,800
Engineers drove four locomotives with four loaded freight cars halfway across the span.

306
00:23:56,440 --> 00:24:01,400
Barring its collapse, they remained there for two hours while structural bend was measured.

307
00:24:01,401 --> 00:24:07,480
Then, they crossed back and forth, first slowly, then to 15 miles per hour.

308
00:24:07,980 --> 00:24:09,920
The Aub River Bridge held.

309
00:24:10,800 --> 00:24:13,940
Siberia had made a joke out of building quick.

310
00:24:14,360 --> 00:24:17,920
Vast distances and harsh seas had stalled the supply lines.

311
00:24:18,380 --> 00:24:22,000
It also brought up the worst of engineering based on economy.

312
00:24:22,460 --> 00:24:28,700
Typically, 3,000 ties were used to a mile, but the Trans-Siberian used 2,000 to save money.

313
00:24:28,701 --> 00:24:33,040
When combined with a light iron rail, the result was disaster.

314
00:24:33,260 --> 00:24:37,240
If you build a light railroad, you need lots of cross ties that hold it up.

315
00:24:37,460 --> 00:24:42,340
This light rail has to bridge from tie to tie, and you get an engine on it.

316
00:24:42,520 --> 00:24:43,880
It's going to sag and stretch.

317
00:24:44,180 --> 00:24:46,880
Also, Siberia has a very severe climate,

318
00:24:47,040 --> 00:24:49,560
and there's a temperature point at which steel becomes brittle,

319
00:24:50,260 --> 00:24:52,080
and they hit that in Siberia.

320
00:24:52,420 --> 00:24:55,220
So the rails would just pop apart and crumble.

321
00:24:55,221 --> 00:25:01,480
The broken pieces of the railroad were patched back together and work resumed.

322
00:25:02,320 --> 00:25:06,160
Even a bad train, argued Vita, is better than no train.

323
00:25:07,360 --> 00:25:09,920
But in October, tragedy struck.

324
00:25:10,840 --> 00:25:18,880
Alexander III, the man who fired a committee for telling him the Trans-Siberian could not be built, died in a train derailment.

325
00:25:18,881 --> 00:25:25,940
Sergei Vita was made a scapegoat and forced to resign by his enemies.

326
00:25:27,060 --> 00:25:29,640
The railroad had lost its two greatest champions.

327
00:25:30,640 --> 00:25:33,380
It fell on Nicholas to complete his father's vision.

328
00:25:36,580 --> 00:25:40,240
The summer in Siberia is a gift of warmth and light.

329
00:25:41,020 --> 00:25:44,360
In Irkosk, people spend long evenings by the Angara River,

330
00:25:44,840 --> 00:25:47,240
mindful that these are the best days of the year.

331
00:25:48,040 --> 00:25:53,880
In six months, temperatures will drop to 10 degrees below zero, and the sun will set at 3 o'clock.

332
00:25:55,180 --> 00:25:58,280
But winter is Siberia's defining season.

333
00:25:59,480 --> 00:26:04,400
For the train to succeed, it had to conquer the climate and sheer size of the continent.

334
00:26:06,340 --> 00:26:10,960
By the fall of 1898, it was better than halfway to victory.

335
00:26:12,080 --> 00:26:16,200
The Trans-Siberian reached Irkutsk in September 1898.

336
00:26:17,240 --> 00:26:23,200
Seven years after the project began, the largest city in Siberia was linked to the capital.

337
00:26:24,200 --> 00:26:32,840
The distance of 2,800 miles was longer than the American Transcontinental Railroad, but still less than halfway across Siberia.

338
00:26:34,740 --> 00:26:35,440
In St.

339
00:26:35,441 --> 00:26:44,620
Petersburg, Tsar Nicholas honored his late father with the title, Most Auguste Founder of the Great Siberian Railway, and erected a statue in his memory.

340
00:26:47,240 --> 00:26:53,940
The progress was a major achievement, not because new technologies had been invented

341
00:26:53,941 --> 00:26:57,000
or a thorny engineering problem solved.

342
00:26:57,001 --> 00:27:02,300
The Trans-Siberians' heroic distinction is that a poor country succeeded in building

343
00:27:02,301 --> 00:27:08,020
a railway across Siberia, albeit one that was markedly behind the technological standard

344
00:27:08,021 --> 00:27:10,380
of the day.

345
00:27:10,381 --> 00:27:18,100
They were building a railroad by hand across the subarctic Siberia at the same time that

346
00:27:18,101 --> 00:27:21,300
the Wright brothers were dreaming of powered flight.

347
00:27:21,301 --> 00:27:26,300
At the very same time that in the US the New York Central Railroad exceeded 100 miles an

348
00:27:26,301 --> 00:27:27,300
hour.

349
00:27:27,301 --> 00:27:33,240
The Russians are aspiring to a railroad that would operate at 10 or 15 miles an hour,

350
00:27:33,241 --> 00:27:36,760
take several weeks to cross the continent.

351
00:27:36,761 --> 00:27:42,440
from the royal celebration, the real heroes, the men who laid the tracks, continued to work,

352
00:27:42,880 --> 00:27:45,827
unheralded and invisible to the national eye.

353
00:27:45,828 --> 00:27:48,840
They drove the railroad forward day after day,

354
00:27:49,180 --> 00:27:51,844
overcoming whatever is in the railroad's path.

355
00:27:51,845 --> 00:27:54,740
That included a forest as large as the continental

356
00:27:54,741 --> 00:27:55,668
United States.

357
00:27:55,669 --> 00:28:01,040
The birch, pine, and spruce were so dense, sunlight had never reached the ground.

358
00:28:01,041 --> 00:28:07,880
When the sun finally touched the frozen forest floor, it turned it into a boundless swamp.

359
00:28:08,760 --> 00:28:14,660
The men had no choice but to work in hip-deep water and wallow in mud until drainage canals were built.

360
00:28:15,420 --> 00:28:18,100
The work moved slowly, but it never stopped.

361
00:28:19,280 --> 00:28:25,060
By 1900, the railroad was finished, except for a final link less than 200 miles long.

362
00:28:25,980 --> 00:28:29,240
The last stretch, joining the western and eastern sections,

363
00:28:29,600 --> 00:28:34,080
was in Central Asia at Lake Baikal, the world's largest body of fresh water.

364
00:28:35,180 --> 00:28:39,180
The lake is 400 miles long and 60 miles across.

365
00:28:39,880 --> 00:28:44,320
It also reaches depths of over a mile, making it the deepest lake on Earth.

366
00:28:45,360 --> 00:28:49,660
Cliffs and mountains rim the southern and eastern shores like the teeth of a shark.

367
00:28:51,000 --> 00:28:53,980
The landscape was a construction crew's nightmare.

368
00:28:55,060 --> 00:29:01,700
Of the route's 6,000 miles, most had crossed Great Plains like the American Midwest or forests and swamps.

369
00:29:02,420 --> 00:29:06,340
But this section was pain and heartache embodied in every mile.

370
00:29:07,220 --> 00:29:13,300
Out of this terrain, crews had to blast a shelf wide enough for the tracks and tunnel through a mountain chain.

371
00:29:14,100 --> 00:29:19,300
The cost was $15 million, six times that of an average mile.

372
00:29:21,860 --> 00:29:24,180
The Railroad Committee's decision was unanimous.

373
00:29:24,820 --> 00:29:26,700
There had to be an easier way.

374
00:29:28,180 --> 00:29:31,580
The Railroad Committee decided to avoid the route through the mountains

375
00:29:31,581 --> 00:29:35,120
and build a train ferry to cross the waters of Lake Baikal.

376
00:29:35,540 --> 00:29:37,520
The idea is copied from the Americans.

377
00:29:38,900 --> 00:29:40,980
This is not an unusual thing to do.

378
00:29:41,680 --> 00:29:45,480
It was used a lot in the United States in the early days,

379
00:29:45,481 --> 00:29:49,220
even until the 1970s, to get trains across Lake Michigan

380
00:29:49,221 --> 00:29:51,960
to avoid the congestion of Chicago.

381
00:29:51,961 --> 00:29:57,360
But at Lake Baikal, what they did was propose a ferry to take the train across,

382
00:29:57,361 --> 00:30:02,520
because they would be quicker than to build the line all the way around the south end of the lake.

383
00:30:04,180 --> 00:30:07,440
The vessel that would cross the lake was named the Baikal.

384
00:30:08,280 --> 00:30:13,460
It was designed and built in England, 297 feet long by 57 feet wide.

385
00:30:14,220 --> 00:30:19,820
It had twin propellers, an inch-thick steel hull reinforced by a two-foot inner sheathing of timber,

386
00:30:19,821 --> 00:30:22,580
and a bow propeller to shear away ice.

387
00:30:23,420 --> 00:30:27,080
When the ship was finished, it broke through ice four feet thick.

388
00:30:27,940 --> 00:30:31,300
It was one of the biggest and most powerful train ferries in the world,

389
00:30:31,980 --> 00:30:35,580
built to carry 1,200 passengers, 28 fully loaded boxcars,

390
00:30:36,580 --> 00:30:39,780
and 12 locomotives at a speed of 13 knots.

391
00:30:41,020 --> 00:30:43,700
Three rows of tracks rested above the ship's keel

392
00:30:43,701 --> 00:30:45,900
so a train could drive on and off the ferry.

393
00:30:45,901 --> 00:30:50,200
It was the one state-of-the-art item on the Trans-Siberian Railway.

394
00:30:51,440 --> 00:30:55,760
A smaller icebreaker and passenger ferry named the Angara was also built.

395
00:30:56,140 --> 00:30:59,660
Its job was to plow ahead of the Baikal, breaking a path for it.

396
00:31:00,600 --> 00:31:03,260
The train ferry was finished in 12 months,

397
00:31:03,680 --> 00:31:07,760
but the only way to get the vessel to Siberia and Lake Baikal

398
00:31:07,761 --> 00:31:12,200
was to break it down, box it, ship it by boat, then rail,

399
00:31:12,201 --> 00:31:15,620
and reassemble it at its new port, Litvianca.

400
00:31:15,900 --> 00:31:20,900
Reaching a landlocked port in Central Asia proved to be more difficult than construction.

401
00:31:20,901 --> 00:31:27,900
The Baikal was delayed by two years when its supply train had to wait until a bridge was constructed.

402
00:31:27,901 --> 00:31:31,900
In the drawn out delay, parts were lost in transit.

403
00:31:31,901 --> 00:31:40,900
Then it was discovered that some ship pieces were too big to fit on the supply train and had to be rebuilt, causing a further loss of time.

404
00:31:40,901 --> 00:31:42,180
It had 15 boilers.

405
00:31:42,620 --> 00:31:45,260
The reason for that is that they couldn't have any one of these boilers

406
00:31:45,261 --> 00:31:48,740
any bigger than would fit on a railroad car to carry it down the track

407
00:31:48,741 --> 00:31:52,340
and get it to Irkutsk and finally get it to the place where the boat was being assembled.

408
00:31:52,700 --> 00:31:57,240
Three more years passed before the ship was finally reassembled and put into service.

409
00:31:57,920 --> 00:32:01,260
But the waste of time was a minor inconvenience

410
00:32:01,261 --> 00:32:04,020
compared to what was in store for the Baikal.

411
00:32:04,700 --> 00:32:08,460
Perhaps one of the biggest surprises in all of Siberia was Lake Baikal.

412
00:32:09,140 --> 00:32:13,520
Even the Russians underestimated winter in Siberia, winter on Lake Baikal.

413
00:32:13,940 --> 00:32:16,340
The lake seemed to have a life of its own.

414
00:32:16,980 --> 00:32:20,280
It had its own weather. It had its own moods.

415
00:32:20,320 --> 00:32:23,000
It had its own hazards and its own beauties.

416
00:32:23,800 --> 00:32:25,860
Storms could come up almost instantly,

417
00:32:26,260 --> 00:32:30,220
storms large enough to throw this immense car ferry, this iron boat,

418
00:32:30,820 --> 00:32:32,720
big enough to sail any ocean in the world,

419
00:32:33,040 --> 00:32:36,520
far enough off course and stop it in its wake.

420
00:32:37,260 --> 00:32:41,460
After one year on the lake, the Baikal proved itself useless.

421
00:32:42,100 --> 00:32:43,520
It was a total loss.

422
00:32:44,000 --> 00:32:49,520
In spring, summer, and fall, storms or fog constantly held the Baikal in port.

423
00:32:49,980 --> 00:32:52,500
In winter, it was unable to break through the ice.

424
00:32:52,501 --> 00:32:57,480
The hydrographers assigned to measure the thickness of the ice had drawn incorrect measurements.

425
00:32:57,800 --> 00:33:01,540
In the dead of winter, the ice rose to nine feet, not four.

426
00:33:02,100 --> 00:33:06,840
In futile desperation, men were sent onto the ice to hack a path for the ferry.

427
00:33:07,180 --> 00:33:08,100
But it was useless.

428
00:33:08,380 --> 00:33:11,720
This whole huge bloody ship slides up on top of the ice,

429
00:33:11,920 --> 00:33:14,080
fails to break through, slides back.

430
00:33:14,820 --> 00:33:16,600
I mean, it just flat doesn't work.

431
00:33:17,460 --> 00:33:22,440
And so passengers and cargoes and stuff are piling up at either side of the lake.

432
00:33:23,020 --> 00:33:24,460
You just physically can't get across.

433
00:33:24,461 --> 00:33:27,820
It's just, you know, a bloody, ill-conceived mess.

434
00:33:27,821 --> 00:33:32,980
Your whole railroad from Moscow to Vladivostok ceases to be able to run.

435
00:33:33,780 --> 00:33:36,440
Lake Baikal had the final word.

436
00:33:37,220 --> 00:33:38,960
The ferries were abandoned in a year,

437
00:33:39,500 --> 00:33:43,860
a frost-rimmed memento of Siberia's power over men and machines.

438
00:33:44,640 --> 00:33:49,460
The Railroad Committee had no alternative but to build a 162-mile track

439
00:33:49,461 --> 00:33:51,980
through the mountains and cliffs above the lake.

440
00:33:52,760 --> 00:33:56,620
The undertaking rivaled the most difficult stretch of track ever built.

441
00:33:56,621 --> 00:34:01,880
It required no less than 38 tunnels through sheer granite mountains.

442
00:34:02,900 --> 00:34:08,960
The only relief was that at long last, the Trans-Siberian was almost finished.

443
00:34:12,700 --> 00:34:18,660
Latvostok, Russia's easternmost city, 6,000 miles and eight time zones from Moscow.

444
00:34:18,661 --> 00:34:26,660
It is the hub of the Pacific Fleet, even though the warships are less at sea and more at anchor in the post-Cold War era.

445
00:34:29,360 --> 00:34:32,500
In Soviet times, it was a restricted city.

446
00:34:33,220 --> 00:34:36,900
Without government permission, no one was allowed to enter or leave.

447
00:34:37,580 --> 00:34:39,300
But those days are over.

448
00:34:40,040 --> 00:34:46,820
The Trans-Siberian Railroad reaches Vladivostok, uninspected by secret police, eight days after it leaves the capital.

449
00:34:47,780 --> 00:34:55,300
It arrives at the station where young Prince Nicholas inaugurated the project in 1891 amid so much hope and fanfare.

450
00:34:56,040 --> 00:35:02,460
A decade after construction began, the Trans-Siberian was once again steeped in excited anticipation.

451
00:35:04,740 --> 00:35:07,080
Crews prepared to drive the railroad to completion.

452
00:35:07,380 --> 00:35:15,400
But first, they had to challenge the formidable cliffs and mountains around it, turning rock into a railway 162 miles long.

453
00:35:16,820 --> 00:35:23,800
This section of track surpassed any of the challenges that once confronted the builders of America's Transcontinental Railroad.

454
00:35:25,320 --> 00:35:31,400
Not only was it more difficult by virtue of the landscape, it had none of America's state-of-the-art equipment.

455
00:35:33,140 --> 00:35:44,160
The most difficult construction on the Trans-Siberian, and arguably in all of railroading, had to rely instead on brute strength, determination, and hand tools.

456
00:35:44,900 --> 00:35:49,280
That's all crews had to build 38 tunnels through a mountain chain

457
00:35:49,281 --> 00:35:54,880
that rises 1,500 feet above a lake the size of an ocean in central Siberia.

458
00:35:55,800 --> 00:35:59,080
Elsewhere in the world, at the beginning of the 20th century,

459
00:35:59,920 --> 00:36:02,140
tunneling was considered high technology.

460
00:36:02,540 --> 00:36:05,540
There were pneumatic drills, there were high explosives,

461
00:36:06,240 --> 00:36:09,380
there were tunneling machines invented in Europe and the United States

462
00:36:09,381 --> 00:36:13,100
that often made fairly short work of solid rock.

463
00:36:14,060 --> 00:36:17,700
Russia built these tunnels the hard way, the old-fashioned way,

464
00:36:17,800 --> 00:36:21,540
with hand drills, with sweaty men with hammers,

465
00:36:21,660 --> 00:36:25,080
working inside cramped, rocky tombs,

466
00:36:25,800 --> 00:36:30,060
making holes in the rock into which the blasters would tamp their powder,

467
00:36:30,420 --> 00:36:34,560
insert the dynamite sticks, and light the fuses and run as fast as they could.

468
00:36:35,220 --> 00:36:37,940
This was tunneling 1850 style.

469
00:36:38,420 --> 00:36:40,700
The trouble was, this was the early 20th century.

470
00:36:40,701 --> 00:36:47,600
In spite of the hardships in building the link past Lake Baikal, the railroad inched onward to completion.

471
00:36:48,560 --> 00:36:55,880
Builders estimated the railroad's finale in the spring of 1904, 14 years after it began in Vladivostok.

472
00:36:57,480 --> 00:36:58,180
In St.

473
00:36:58,181 --> 00:37:06,020
Petersburg, Tsar Nicholas II eagerly awaited the train's successful completion with all the pride and excitement of a new father.

474
00:37:06,021 --> 00:37:17,660
With the last mile only months away, he prepared a celebration, boasting to a world audience that the railroad had bridled Asia in steam and iron at last.

475
00:37:18,320 --> 00:37:27,760
He also lavished the railroad in bravado, flaunting it as a tool of empire and bragging it marked a new era of Russian military prowess in the Far East.

476
00:37:28,440 --> 00:37:32,200
The Tsar would have been better off to say nothing at all.

477
00:37:32,201 --> 00:37:37,160
His unmeasured speech and lack of tact sparked a cataclysmic disaster.

478
00:37:37,960 --> 00:37:43,060
On February 8th, 1904, Japan launched a sneak attack on Russia's Pacific coast

479
00:37:43,061 --> 00:37:48,560
to destroy the Trans-Siberian Railroad and break St. Petersburg's link to the east.

480
00:37:49,300 --> 00:37:53,960
The Japanese deliberately struck in the dead of winter when the train was most vulnerable.

481
00:37:54,540 --> 00:38:01,460
But to Russia and the Tsar's horror, the Trans-Siberian was not just vulnerable up and down at 6,000 miles.

482
00:38:01,461 --> 00:38:03,460
It was a death trap.

483
00:38:03,461 --> 00:38:05,460
Its light rails broke down,

484
00:38:05,461 --> 00:38:07,460
unable to bear the weight of boxcars

485
00:38:07,461 --> 00:38:09,460
overloaded with weapons and soldiers.

486
00:38:09,461 --> 00:38:11,460
There was a great line about the Trans-Siberian

487
00:38:11,461 --> 00:38:13,460
that kind of got coined fairly early,

488
00:38:13,461 --> 00:38:16,460
and that is a first-rate, third-rate railroad.

489
00:38:16,461 --> 00:38:19,460
And the third-rate part meant just everything was cheap.

490
00:38:19,461 --> 00:38:23,460
They would begin to do the grading instead of a sturdy basis.

491
00:38:23,461 --> 00:38:25,460
You know, it'd be a pile of dirt,

492
00:38:25,461 --> 00:38:29,460
and cheap green wood for the ties.

493
00:38:29,461 --> 00:38:33,560
pretty cheap and soft iron for the rails.

494
00:38:34,240 --> 00:38:35,780
And you get a railroad for a while,

495
00:38:35,920 --> 00:38:38,720
and then come derailments, come washouts.

496
00:38:39,680 --> 00:38:41,720
I mean, there was a derailment every few weeks.

497
00:38:42,940 --> 00:38:45,620
The policy of building quick and cheap

498
00:38:45,621 --> 00:38:47,900
was a disaster in the exigency of war.

499
00:38:50,180 --> 00:38:53,720
They built it, first of all, single-tracked to save money,

500
00:38:53,721 --> 00:38:55,920
with the result that during the war

501
00:38:55,921 --> 00:39:04,500
They couldn't send troops to China at the same time that they were bringing wounded troops out of China.

502
00:39:04,900 --> 00:39:06,620
The lines were constantly clogged.

503
00:39:07,200 --> 00:39:11,380
Within 10 years of the initial construction, they began reconstructing the entire railroad.

504
00:39:12,060 --> 00:39:16,260
The war ended a year later in defeat for the Tsar and the railroad.

505
00:39:17,040 --> 00:39:21,480
The Trans-Siberian was blamed as one of the reasons for Russia's ignominious defeat.

506
00:39:22,140 --> 00:39:24,500
A painstaking process of rebuilding began.

507
00:39:24,501 --> 00:39:29,500
Finally, by 1916, the railroad was finished.

508
00:39:29,501 --> 00:39:33,500
Russia had its long-desired transcontinental railroad,

509
00:39:33,501 --> 00:39:35,500
but there was no celebration.

510
00:39:35,501 --> 00:39:37,500
Well, I suppose in one way you could call building this railroad

511
00:39:37,501 --> 00:39:40,500
like the Thirty Years' War. It's a great dream.

512
00:39:40,501 --> 00:39:43,500
It obviously has lots of aspects of empire.

513
00:39:43,501 --> 00:39:47,500
But in the end, it becomes this connection between a nation

514
00:39:47,501 --> 00:39:51,500
whose geography is so vast that by the time they finally get to the end

515
00:39:51,501 --> 00:39:55,534
to the end a decade later. Everyone is just exhausted.

516
00:39:55,535 --> 00:39:58,000
The railroad and the war depleted

517
00:39:58,001 --> 00:39:59,474
the national treasury.

518
00:39:59,475 --> 00:40:04,300
There was no capital to fund social programs, making bad times in Russia

519
00:40:04,301 --> 00:40:08,913
worse. It also prevented the government from modernizing the army.

520
00:40:08,914 --> 00:40:10,800
When World War I broke out,

521
00:40:11,300 --> 00:40:16,780
Russia was ill-equipped and suffered horrible casualties. The troubles at the front,

522
00:40:16,781 --> 00:40:23,400
Its problems at home led to an insurmountable crisis and ultimately the Russian Revolution.

523
00:40:24,280 --> 00:40:31,940
The promise of the railroad to bring peace and prosperity, once used to justify its enormous ambition, went unfulfilled.

524
00:40:33,220 --> 00:40:38,220
There was almost a utopian vision behind this railroad that it would solve all of the problems of Russia,

525
00:40:38,640 --> 00:40:44,300
that it would bring Russia into the center of world trade, and none of it came to pass.

526
00:40:44,301 --> 00:40:50,709
Nicholas II, who proudly inaugurated the railroad's construction, had his final glimpse of St.

527
00:40:50,710 --> 00:40:53,300
Petersburg from a window on the train.

528
00:40:53,301 --> 00:41:03,300
Under cover of darkness, the Trans-Siberian Railroad, once the hope of the Romanov family, carried Nicholas II to his death in Siberia.

529
00:41:03,301 --> 00:41:11,300
In years that followed, the Tsar's once rickety train grew into a mighty railroad when money and time were added to improve it.

530
00:41:14,300 --> 00:41:19,980
In the Russian Civil War, the Communists used the Trans-Siberian to consolidate power across the country.

531
00:41:21,000 --> 00:41:26,780
During World War II, it rushed fresh soldiers to the battlefronts, turning the tide of the war against Hitler.

532
00:41:28,180 --> 00:41:34,780
In the 1950s, the industrial might of the Soviet Union turned the railroad into a state-of-the-art masterpiece.

533
00:41:34,781 --> 00:41:45,180
By the end of the decade, the Communists upgraded the Trans-Siberian with heavy steel rails, new cross-tiles, new bridges, and a second track.

534
00:41:46,060 --> 00:41:49,860
The result is one of the world's strongest and most durable railways.

535
00:41:50,480 --> 00:42:00,740
This was in every way a handmade, hard-won, difficult project that took a toll in time and money and blood.

536
00:42:00,741 --> 00:42:06,940
The railway the Tsar desperately wanted, but could barely afford,

537
00:42:07,420 --> 00:42:11,960
had fulfilled its promise a century after the first crews struggled to build it.

538
00:42:12,860 --> 00:42:18,300
A relic of a time gone by, when strength on strength, more than any single ingredient,

539
00:42:18,900 --> 00:42:23,600
triumphed in building the world's longest railroad across a hostile wilderness.

540
00:42:24,840 --> 00:42:29,460
Get ready for war under the command of Moses, Abraham, King David.

541
00:42:29,461 --> 00:42:33,000
You wouldn't think it, but these were the men who led thousands into battle.

542
00:42:33,220 --> 00:42:36,100
With the strike of the sword, you would hear the screams of terror.

543
00:42:36,380 --> 00:42:37,760
This is all very bloody business.

544
00:42:38,020 --> 00:42:40,840
It's time to examine the Old Testament from a military perspective.

545
00:42:41,220 --> 00:42:43,700
Bible Battles, tonight at 10 on History.

